    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d559">

                <pb facs="0696" n="584" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg584"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/569a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-05-21T17:00:00-04:00">May 21, 1935—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. Press of May 20 contains news items under Nanking date line of May 19 to the
                    effect that Dr. Ma Yin-chu<anchor n="96" xml:id="d559-f1"/> is urging, as the
                    most effective means of combating the American silver purchasing policy, a
                    Chinese boycott of American goods.</p>
                <p>Legation and consulates should follow situation closely and report promptly by
                    telegram to Department any manifestations of anti-American feeling growing out
                    of the American silver program and particularly any developments tending toward
                    a boycott of American goods.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d559-f1" n="96" xml:id="d559-f1-content">
                <p>Chinese economist and member of Legislative Yuan since 1928.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
